<BaseLayout title="About — Latent Form Theory">
  <header class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Maker Behind the Method</p>

    <h1>
      Jason Deters
    </h1>

    <p class="lead">
      An artist and craftsman working at the intersection of discipline, natural material, and subtractive philosophy.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>A Discipline of Attention</h2>
    <p>
      Latent Form Theory was not conceived in a vacuum; it was forged through over thirty years of exacting graphic design, custom printing management, and hands-on structural composition. For Jason Deters, the journey to essential subtraction has always been about understanding how a medium communicates under constraint.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, that dialogue is physical. Working individually out of his North Carolina studio, Jason designs and handcrafts high-end, minimalist furniture and objects. Rejecting the modern tendency to impose arbitrary, standardized shapes onto raw matter, his process relies on a deep listening to the physical properties of the wood. 
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Honoring the Material</h2>
    <p>
      Whether working with the straight, quiet character of premium quarter-sawn white oak, the deep warmth of walnut, or the vivid grain of purpleheart, Jason’s pieces are defined by a quiet restraint. By finishing each piece with premium natural hardwax oils rather than heavy synthetic lacquers, the tactile warmth and physical history of the wood remain entirely legible.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a world of constant accumulation, his work stands as an invitation to ask for less—allowing shadow, joinery, and the quiet beauty of natural structure to speak for themselves.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>